.TH HD-DOCKER 7 "October 2026" "hd-docker" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-docker \- Docker on HyprDebian
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-docker
installs Docker, from the package
.BR docker.io ,
with its command line, from
.BR docker-cli ,
and buildx, which
.B docker build
uses, from
.BR docker-buildx ,
and Docker Compose, from
.BR docker-compose ,
and sets them up to work with the
.BR ufw (8)
firewall, which it also depends on.
.PP
To use Docker without sudo, add your user to the group
.B docker
and log in again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo usermod \-a \-G docker <user>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Members of that group control Docker, and so the whole system.
.SH ADDRESSES
.I /etc/docker/daemon.json
gives the bridge
.B docker0
the address 192.168.253.1/24, and the networks that Docker makes, such as
those of
.BR "docker compose" ,
a /24 each from 192.168.240.0/20.
This keeps them clear of the usual home and office networks.
.PP
The file is a conffile of
.BR hd-docker .
Restart Docker after changing it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo systemctl restart docker
.fi
.RE
.PP
When you change the address pool, change the
.B RETURN
rule of the firewall block to match; see
.BR FIREWALL .
.SH OPEN FILES
.I /etc/docker/daemon.json
also gives containers a limit of 1024 open files, which a container may raise
to at most 524288.
Without it, the containers of some versions of containerd, such as the one of
trixie, may open about a billion files, and every command that closes all the
files it may have open before it starts another, as apt does, then takes
minutes.
An upgrade from a version of
.B hd-docker
before 0.3 does not restart Docker, so the limit only applies after a restart
of Docker; the upgrade says so.
.PP
The first installation of
.B hd-docker
restarts Docker, which
.B docker.io
started with its default addresses; later installations do not, as a restart
stops the containers that run.
.SH FIREWALL
Docker publishes the ports of containers with rules of its own, which come
before those of ufw, so a published port would be open whatever ufw says.
.B hd-docker
puts rules in the chain
.B DOCKER-USER
that Docker checks first:
.IP \(bu 2
replies to connections that a container made, or that were allowed, pass;
.IP \(bu 2
the route rules of ufw are checked, so a container is reached only when one
of them allows it;
.IP \(bu 2
traffic from the networks of Docker, 192.168.240.0/20, passes;
.IP \(bu 2
all other traffic to the containers is dropped.
.PP
To open a published port, allow it with a route rule.
Docker has already sent the traffic on to the container by then, so the rule
names the port inside the container, not the published one: for
.BR "\-p 8080:80" ,
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo ufw route allow proto tcp to any port 80
.fi
.RE
.PP
The rules are in a block at the end of
.IR /etc/ufw/after.rules ,
between the lines
.B # BEGIN DOCKER UFW FIX
and
.BR "# END DOCKER UFW FIX" .
That file belongs to ufw, so
.B hd-docker
does not ship it: every installation and upgrade puts the block in from
.I /usr/share/hd-docker/docker-ufw.rules
and keeps the rest of the file, and ufw is reloaded when the block changed and
the firewall is on.
Changes made inside the block are lost then; put your own rules outside it.
Removing the package takes the block out again.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/docker/daemon.json
The addresses of the networks of Docker and the open-files limit of
containers, a conffile.
.TP
.I /etc/ufw/after.rules
The rules of ufw, with the block of
.BR hd-docker .
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-docker/docker-ufw.rules
The block that goes in
.IR /etc/ufw/after.rules .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR docker (1),
.BR docker-compose (1),
.BR ufw (8),
.BR ufw-framework (8)
